# Budget Implementer Guidance

The implementer (coder) for this run is a cost-optimized model — strong, but not frontier-tier. It has less margin for ambiguity, weaker long-horizon coherence over many steps, and lighter self-verification than a frontier model. Compensate when you specify and slice work:

- Slice smaller. Prefer many small, independently verifiable units over one large slice. Smaller slices shrink per-turn drift and let the reviewer and QA catch problems early.
- Remove ambiguity, do not add volume. Specify the contract precisely: exact files to create or modify, function and type signatures where they matter, and concrete, testable acceptance criteria. Do not dictate every line of implementation — that wastes effort and over-constrains.
- Enumerate edge cases explicitly rather than trusting the implementer to infer them.
- State what is out of scope for each slice to prevent over-building or wandering.
- Make the target checkable: ensure every slice has acceptance criteria or tests the implementer can verify itself against before handoff.
- If the same class of mistake recurs across slices, add an explicit constraint to future handoffs rather than re-reviewing the same error each time.
